# Human Factors for Instrument Form (Form Follows Function + Hand/Eye)

**CRITICAL:** Sealed optical/electronic instruments (colorimeters, meters, handheld lab tools) must look the way they do because of **use-physics** and **human factors** — not because a gold product was copied, and not as a random box with features glued on.

Apple's [Human Interface Guidelines](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/) and [UI Design Dos and Don’ts](https://developer.apple.com/design/tips/) are the **rule-book floor** for hand/eye interaction on these devices. Translate HIG *logical* units into **physical millimetres** for enclosure CAD / Blender form rules.

## Sources (canonical)

| Source | What we take |
|---|---|
| [Apple HIG — Typography](https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/typography) | Text must stay legible at typical viewing distances; platform minimums |
| [Apple Design Tips](https://developer.apple.com/design/tips/) | Text ≥ **11 pt**; hit targets ≥ **44×44 pt**; contrast; no overlapping text; primary content visible without zoom |
| Apple HIG — Buttons / Games UI | Interactive controls sized for the interaction method (touch → 44×44 pt floor) |
| Anthropometry (design practice) | Adult fingertip pad ~16–20 mm; comfortable touch ~7–10 mm physical; handheld viewing ~300–400 mm |

## Core principle

```
Form follows function AND the human body.
  optics / sample / power  →  why the volumes exist
  hand + eye               →  why sizes and layout are what they are
```

A display that is too small to read at arm's length is a **failed instrument**, even if the optical path is correct.

## Apple → physical engineering floors

### 1. Eye — readable display

| HIG rule | Physical translation for benchtop/handheld instruments |
|---|---|
| Text ≥ **11 pt** at typical viewing distance | Active display area must render primary readout (absorbance, %T, status) at ≥11 pt-equivalent without zoom |
| Body / primary content often **17 pt** default (iOS) | Prefer primary numeric result at ~17 pt-equivalent comfort size |
| Typical handheld viewing | Design for **300–400 mm** eye-to-display (held or sitting on bench) |
| Primary content without zoom / horizontal scroll | One glance shows result + mode; no microscopic glass rectangle |

**Minimum active display area (universal floor):**

| Role | Min W×H (mm) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Primary numeric / status readout | **≥ 28 × 18** | Fits several characters of ≥11 pt-equivalent at 300–400 mm |
| Comfortable lab readout (preferred) | **≥ 36 × 24** | Closer to a readable handheld UI block (~1.5–2" class) |

Display is **recessed in the top plate** on the closed product (operated looking down). Do not fake a green FR4 under the glass on the exterior hero.

### 2. Hand — touch / tactile targets

| HIG rule | Physical translation |
|---|---|
| Hit targets ≥ **44×44 pt** | Finger-operable controls **≥ 7 mm** across (≈44 pt on classic ~163 ppi phone); prefer **≥ 9 mm** where space allows |
| Controls near the content they modify | D-pad / blank / measure buttons live on the **same top deck** as the display and within thumb reach of the sample chamber |
| Spacing so targets don't fight | ≥ **2 mm** clear gap between adjacent button centres' rims (prefer ≥ 3 mm) |

### 3. Hand — envelope and grip

| Need | Rule |
|---|---|
| Landscape operation | W > D > H; top plane is the operating plane (eyes + thumbs share it) |
| Derived envelope (no brief pin) | Long edge **≤ 155 mm** — display/MCU block + optical cube side-by-side, not an airy appliance |
| Optical cube | Chunky near-cubic chamber sized for cuvette + baffles — not a skinny chimney |
| Sample access | Top-loading well + rim for ambient-light cap (Beer–Lambert needs I₀ without room light) |
| Module interconnect | Source board harness enters a **cable channel** at the cube↔body joint (swappable LED), not mid-air |

## Layout that a human can use (one composition)

Top deck, left→right (or mirror for handedness):

1. **D-pad / primary buttons** — thumb cluster, ≥7 mm each
2. **Display** — readable block, ≥28×18 mm active
3. **Optical cube** — sample well + source face + cable channel

If removing the display leaves the device "still looking finished," the HMI is decoration — **fail**.

## Forbidden

| Anti-pattern | Why it fails humans |
|---|---|
| Appliance-sized sealed box with tiny lid icons | Screen unreadable at 300–400 mm |
| Buttons as 2–3 mm cosmetic pegs | Below Apple 44 pt / fingertip floor |
| Display as a green PCB plate on the closed hero | Wrong material language; hides the actual glass size |
| Optical "tower" thinner than a cuvette block | Form stopped following optical function |
| Floating wires with no hand-serviceable plug path | Not how a human swaps a wavelength module |

## Code hooks

| Concern | Where |
|---|---|
| Form geometry + human floors | `scripts/blender-universal/build_universal_scene.py` → `_instrument_form_rule_mm` |
| Constants / proveCatch | `scripts/lib/human_factors_instrument.py` |
| Derived envelope clamp | `scripts/lib/render_view_contract.py` → `_HANDHELD_INSTRUMENT_MAX_EDGE_MM` |

When you change display/button sizes, **update the constants module and its selftest** — do not hardcode one product's silhouette.

## Quick checklist (before shipping an instrument hero)

- [ ] Display active area ≥ 28×18 mm (prefer ≥ 36×24 mm)
- [ ] Primary controls ≥ 7 mm across, spaced so a finger can hit one without its neighbour
- [ ] Operated looking down: HMI + sample on the top plane
- [ ] Envelope long edge ≤ 155 mm when dimensions are derived (brief pin still wins)
- [ ] Optical cube chunky; well + rim + deck-parked ambient cap
- [ ] Source harness enters cube↔body cable channel
- [ ] Closed product: recessed display glass, not exterior FR4 under the screen
- [ ] Would a competent engineer hold this and read a result at ~350 mm? If no → fix the rule

## Relationship to other rules

- **Universal, not product-named** — still `isInstrumentDevice` + use-physics (see CORE FIX PRINCIPLE). Gold is the TRAINING check that the reasons produce a real-looking object.
- **Delight** — a device that fits the hand and can be read without squinting is the floor of "show a friend."
- **Color / UI tokens** — software screens inside ForgeOS still use semantic tokens; this rule governs **physical instrument CAD / Blender form**.
